That doesn&#x27;t follow. Predictability is your brain reacting to the lack of novelty. What an LLM produces is a chain of words following some kind of conditional distribution, whose output could vary significantly from your understanding of novelty depending on its training and priors.<p>With a single, generalized prompt (&quot;produce something unpredictable&quot;), ChatGPT responded with:<p>&gt; 00:— &#x2F; ANOMALOUS RESIDUE  
&gt; Vekh.  
&gt; Ìssara.  
&gt; Nnnn—tq—èl.  
&gt;   
&gt; A temporary thickness enters the meat of the signal.  
&gt;   
&gt; Not rain. Not a substance with permission to fall.  
&gt;   
&gt; UL &#x2F; 9 &#x2F; ferrous &#x2F; ∂  
&gt;      The measurements are eating each other.  
&gt;   
&gt; Someone has left an entire childhood in the wrong unit of pressure.<p>That is not predictable to me, at least. It could be boring, but I think that is mainly a product of me knowing where the text is from. LLM-generated text is inherently boring, to me, but that has nothing to do with predictability.

&gt; There are tools out there that claim to be able to tell the difference between LLM-written prose and the old-fashioned biological product. I&#x27;m wary of them, partly due skeptism about their accuracy, but also because it rather misses the point<p>HN uses automatic classifiers. They seem pretty effective. At the same time, I think it&#x27;s a loosing battle. I&#x27;d rather HN either flag for others to ignore&#x2F;filter&#x2F;review if they want to, and&#x2F;or allow people to self-report (ala TikTok)<p>Eg. I wouldn&#x27;t mind if a comment on HN was like:<p>Hey everyone, I coded this little thing:<p>&lt;ai-generated&gt;
some code
&lt;&#x2F;ai-generated&gt;<p>Then users can choose if they want to see posts with AI-generated stuff or not, or something else...<p>Of course there are always going to be some people trying to game the system. But what are we optimizing for? (a lot of times the 95% unnecessarily suffers because of the mitigation efforts to prevent the other 5% from abuse)
